---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import GateDiagram from '../../components/GateDiagram.astro';
import Histogram from '../../components/Histogram.astro';
import ThoughtProcess from '../../components/ThoughtProcess.astro';
import type { Metric } from '../../data/projects';

const repo = 'https://github.com/omkar-k0/guidance-reconciliation-engine';
const src = (f: string) => `${repo}/blob/main/src/gre/${f}`;

const metrics: Metric[] = [
  { value: '185', label: 'earnings releases read, 11 companies, Jan 2022 to Oct 2026', evidence: 'measured', source: 'data/processed/results.json' },
  { value: '7 of 188', label: 'items sent to the LLM (3.7%); the rest decided by rules', evidence: 'measured', source: 'data/processed/results.json, route field' },
  { value: '23', label: 'unit tests on synthetic inputs', evidence: 'measured', source: 'tests/' },
  { value: '97.9%', label: 'accuracy on all 188 items, hand-labelled blind (always-MET baseline: 91.5%)', evidence: 'measured', source: 'gre eval → data/processed/eval.json, 20 Nov 2026' },
];

const facts = [
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Product' },
  { label: 'When', value: 'October 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Sole developer: data, rules, gate, LLM layer, site' },
  { label: 'Data', value: 'SEC 8-K earnings releases and XBRL company facts' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Live · 97.9% on hand labels' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain language' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'obvious', label: 'Why the obvious fails' },
  { id: 'gate', label: 'The gate' },
  { id: 'system', label: 'Thought process' },
  { id: 'decisions', label: 'Decisions' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'What came out' },
  { id: 'refuses', label: 'What it refuses to claim' },
  { id: 'broke', label: 'What broke' },
  { id: 'open', label: 'Still open' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Guidance Reconciliation Engine"
  question="Did the company hit the revenue guidance it gave last quarter?"
  description="A confidence-gated engine that checks US tech companies' quarterly revenue guidance against what they later reported, with rules first, an LLM only for the unclear rest, and exact citations."
  crumb="Product · 01"
  links={[
    { label: 'Open it live', href: 'https://guidance-reconciliation-engine.vercel.app', primary: true },
    { label: 'Source on GitHub', href: repo },
  ]}
  metrics={metrics}
  facts={facts}
  toc={toc}
  tldr={{
    problem: 'Companies give revenue guidance every quarter. Checking it against what they later reported means reading hundreds of filings by hand.',
    result: '184 of 188 guidance items right against blind hand labels (97.9%); only 7 (3.7%) needed the LLM. All 110 high-confidence rule decisions were right.',
    role: 'Sole developer: SEC data pipeline, rules, confidence gate, Claude layer and the live site.',
  }}
  reproduce={{
    repo,
    commands: `git clone ${repo} && cd guidance-reconciliation-engine
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
cp .env.example .env      # SEC contact e-mail, Anthropic key
set -a && source .env && set +a
pytest                    # 23 unit tests on synthetic inputs
gre fetch                 # releases and XBRL facts from SEC
gre run --no-llm          # rules only, free
gre run                   # with Claude on the low-confidence rest
gre eval                  # scores against data/labels/worksheet.csv`,
    files: [
      { path: 'data/processed/results.json', what: 'Every reconciled item with its route, confidence and citation' },
      { path: 'data/labels/worksheet.csv', what: 'The 188 blind hand labels, with notes' },
      { path: 'data/processed/eval.json', what: 'Accuracy, by route, confusion table and calibration' },
      { path: 'tests/', what: 'The 23 unit tests' },
    ],
  }}
  stack={['Python', 'SEC EDGAR', 'XBRL', 'Claude Haiku (tool use)', 'pytest', 'Vercel']}
  prev={{ href: '/work/portfolio/', label: 'This website' }}
  next={{ href: '/work/faultline/', label: 'faultline' }}
>
  <section id="plain">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>In plain language</h2>
    <p>
      Every quarter, large companies say how much revenue they expect next quarter. Three months later they
      report what actually happened. This engine reads both from public SEC filings and labels each forecast
      <strong>MET</strong>, <strong>MISSED</strong>, <strong>RESTATED</strong> or <strong>UNVERIFIABLE</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Simple rules handle the clear cases. A language model only looks at the few cases the rules are unsure
      about, and its answer counts only if it quotes the filing word for word. When the evidence is not there,
      the engine says so instead of guessing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="question">
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>The question</h2>
    <p class="callout">
      Can a system decide most of these cases with cheap, transparent rules, hand only the unclear rest to a
      model, and still back every single label with a quote someone can check?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="obvious">
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>Why the obvious approach fails</h2>
    <p>
      There are two obvious ways to build this. Both fail in ways that are easy to miss.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A script with regular expressions</strong> is cheap and fast. It also gives the same flat
      answer for a clean sentence and for a messy one, with no sign of which is which. It will happily
      compare a full-year forecast against a single quarter and report a miss.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An LLM on every filing</strong> reads messy text well. But it always returns an answer,
      written just as confidently when the guided period and the reported period do not match. And it costs
      a model call for every one of the 188 items, including the ones a rule gets right for free.
    </p>
    <p>
      The filings themselves are less tidy than they look. NVIDIA guides "$8.10 billion, plus or minus 2%".
      Texas Instruments gives a range. Broadcom gives an approximate figure. Fiscal years end in different
      months. Many companies never report a fourth quarter on its own, only the full year.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gate">
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>The gate</h2>
    <p>
      The rule layer does not just give a label. It scores how sure it is and writes down every reason it lost
      confidence. That score decides who answers.
    </p>
    <GateDiagram />
    <h3>What lowers the rule layer's confidence</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Deduction</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>No forward-looking word near the figure</td><td class="n">−0.30</td><td>The number might be a past result, not a forecast</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Adjusted basis (currency, exclusions)</td><td class="n">−0.25</td><td>May not compare to reported total revenue</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reported figure within 0.5% of the low end</td><td class="n">−0.20</td><td>MET versus MISSED depends on rounding</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Period not named</td><td class="n">−0.15</td><td>"Next quarter" has to be assumed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Point estimate ("approximately $X")</td><td class="n">−0.15</td><td>No range given; met from 99% of X</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Range written in reverse</td><td class="n">−0.10</td><td>Unusual format, worth a second look</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="caption">From <a href={src('extract.py')}>extract.py</a> and <a href={src('rules.py')}>rules.py</a>. Final confidence is the lower of parsing confidence and matching confidence.</p>
    <p>
      In this run, 6 of the 7 escalations were AMD releases. AMD writes its outlook in a way the rule layer
      does not recognise as forward-looking, so each one lost 0.30 and went to the model. That is the gate
      doing its job: the unusual format got a closer read instead of a silent guess.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="system">
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Thought process</h2>
    <p>Left to right, the path one guidance item takes. The model only appears in the last card, and only for items the rules could not settle.</p>
    <ThoughtProcess
      steps={[
        { title: 'Fetch', points: ['8-K earnings releases, exhibit 99.1', 'XBRL company facts', 'SEC fair-access rules'], takeaway: 'Public filings, nothing scraped from news.' },
        { title: 'Extract', points: ['Outlook blocks, line by line', 'Total revenue only, segments skipped', 'Range, midpoint ± or point estimate'], takeaway: 'Every lost point of confidence is written down.' },
        { title: 'Match', points: ['First quarter ending after the filing', 'Five XBRL revenue tags merged', 'Q4 = full year − nine months'], takeaway: 'Dates, never fiscal labels.' },
        { title: 'Decide', points: ['MET if reported ≥ low end', 'Restatements checked', 'Confidence = min(parse, match)'], takeaway: 'Rules settle 181 of 188.' },
        { title: 'Adjudicate', points: ['Below 0.75 only', 'Typed tool call, four labels', 'Quote must be verbatim'], takeaway: 'No quote, no label.', model: true },
      ]}
      note="The rule layer and the model answer the same question with the same four labels. The difference is who is allowed to answer, and that is decided by confidence."
    />
    <h3>Step by step</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Fetch.</strong> Download 8-K earnings releases (Item 2.02, exhibit 99.1) and XBRL company facts from SEC EDGAR, following SEC fair-access rules.</li>
      <li><strong>Extract.</strong> Find outlook blocks line by line, skip segment rows and full-year guidance, and parse the total-revenue range.</li>
      <li><strong>Match.</strong> Find the reported revenue for the first roughly three-month period ending after the filing date. Merge the five XBRL revenue tags companies switch between. Derive Q4 as full year minus nine months.</li>
      <li><strong>Decide.</strong> Rules label the item and score themselves.</li>
      <li><strong>Gate.</strong> At or above 0.75 the rule decision stands. Below it, Claude gets the filing text and must answer through a typed tool call.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the quote.</strong> Every citation the model gives must appear verbatim in the source. With no valid quote from the guidance, the label becomes UNVERIFIABLE. Answers are cached on disk, so re-runs cost nothing.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2><span class="num">06</span>Decisions, and what they cost</h2>
    <div class="decisions">
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Rules first, LLM for the rest</strong>
          <p>181 of 188 items were decided without a model call. Only 7 needed one.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>An LLM on every item</strong>
          <p>188 calls, and no record of which answers came from easy cases and which from hard ones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>No quote, no label</strong>
          <p>A model answer only counts if it quotes the filing exactly. Anything else becomes UNVERIFIABLE.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Trusting the model's own confidence</strong>
          <p>A confident paragraph is not evidence. A quote someone can find in the filing is.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Match on period end dates</strong>
          <p>Reported period must end after the filing date, within 130 days.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Match on fiscal labels</strong>
          <p>NVIDIA's "first quarter of fiscal 2023" ended in May 2022. Labels across companies do not line up; dates do.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Count out-of-scope cases</strong>
          <p>Full-year, growth-percentage and segment guidance are skipped and counted. Workday was dropped: its guidance is subscription revenue only.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Stretch the rules to cover them</strong>
          <p>Each stretch adds a way to be quietly wrong. v1 stays narrow: quarterly total revenue.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Calibrate the threshold on labels</strong>
          <p>Temperature scaling plus the lowest threshold at which rule-decided items reach 95% precision. Built, waiting for labels.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Pick a threshold that looks right</strong>
          <p>0.75 is a placeholder until hand labels say otherwise.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2><span class="num">07</span>What came out</h2>
    <figure class="shot">
      <a href="https://guidance-reconciliation-engine.vercel.app" rel="noopener">
        <img src="/shots/gre-decisions.webp" width="1380" height="750" loading="lazy"
          alt="The live engine, filtered to items the LLM decided. One AMD quarter is labelled MET with its reasoning; the next is labelled UNVERIFIABLE because the period could not be matched. Each item links to the filing on sec.gov." />
      </a>
      <figcaption class="caption">The live site, filtered to the 7 items the LLM decided. The second one refused because the period label did not match. The hand label says MET, so it counts as one of the 4 errors (section 09).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Of 174 quarters with a reported figure, the engine found 2 where revenue came in below the low end of
      guidance. Both are well-known misses: NVIDIA's second quarter of fiscal 2023 (guided $8.10B ± 2%,
      reported $6.70B) and Micron's fourth quarter of fiscal 2022 (guided $7.2B ± $0.4B, reported $6.64B).
    </p>
    <Histogram />
    <p>
      Everything else landed at or above the low end. 48 quarters landed within 2% above it. For these
      companies, the low end of the guided range is rarely broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      11 items are UNVERIFIABLE because their quarter had not been reported yet when the engine ran on
      24 October 2026. One more is UNVERIFIABLE because the model declined it (see section 09).
    </p>
    <h3>Scored against hand labels</h3>
    <p>
      I labelled all 188 items blind, from the filings, without seeing the engine's answers. Then
      <code>gre eval</code> compared the two.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Slice</th><th>Items</th><th>Right</th><th>Accuracy</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>All items</td><td class="n">188</td><td class="n">184</td><td class="n">97.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Answer "MET" for everything (baseline)</td><td class="n">188</td><td class="n">172</td><td class="n">91.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rules, confidence ≥ 0.95</td><td class="n">110</td><td class="n">110</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rules, confidence 0.75–0.95</td><td class="n">71</td><td class="n">68</td><td class="n">95.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LLM</td><td class="n">7</td><td class="n">6</td><td class="n">85.7%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The baseline row matters: most quarters are beats, so 91.5% is free. The engine's real work is the
      other 16 items, the 2 misses and the 14 that cannot be checked. It got 13 of those 16 right. The 3 it got
      wrong all sit in the lower rule band, which is where the threshold should move (section 10).
    </p>
    <p class="caption">One labeller, no second rater. Labels: data/labels/worksheet.csv. Scores: data/processed/eval.json.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="refuses">
    <h2><span class="num">08</span>What it refuses to claim</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Accuracy beyond these 188 items.</strong> 97.9% is measured on this run: 11 large US tech
      companies whose releases follow a tidy format. Other companies, other formats or other guidance types
      may score lower.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A "beat rate" for these companies.</strong> The chart shows the engine's output on 11 hand-picked
      companies. That describes these releases, not how often companies beat guidance in general.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="broke">
    <h2><span class="num">09</span>What broke</h2>
    <h3>Three items that should not exist</h3>
    <p>
      While writing this page I checked all 188 items by hand. Three come from releases that contain a second
      dollar figure next to the quarterly guidance. The extractor turned each into an extra item:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Release</th><th>What it read</th><th>Label given</th><th>Rule conf.</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cisco, May 2024</td><td>Full-year revenue guidance of $53.6–53.8B, compared against one quarter</td><td>MISSED</td><td class="n">0.80</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Broadcom, Sep 2024</td><td>Full-year AI revenue of $12B, compared against total quarterly revenue</td><td>MET</td><td class="n">0.80</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Broadcom, Mar 2026</td><td>A $10B share buyback, read as revenue guidance</td><td>MET</td><td class="n">0.85</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      All three cleared the gate. The confidence deductions only catch the problems they were written for, and
      "second figure in the same release" was not one of them. The fix is a structural rule, not another
      deduction: one quarterly total-revenue item per release. Hand labelling caught all three, and they are
      3 of the engine's 4 errors.
    </p>
    <h3>The model refused where the rule was right</h3>
    <p>
      For AMD's October 2024 release, the extractor attached the wrong period label to correct guidance. The
      model noticed that the label and the reported period did not agree and returned UNVERIFIABLE. The rule
      layer had said MET, and the hand label agrees with the rule. This is the 4th error. It is the safe kind:
      a refusal, not a wrong answer. But it still counts against accuracy, and the real fix is the period label.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2><span class="num">10</span>Still open</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Enforce one quarterly item per release, and fix AMD's period label.</li>
      <li>Split UNVERIFIABLE into "cannot tell" and "not reported yet".</li>
      <li>RESTATED never fired in this run. The detection exists but has not been seen working on real data.</li>
      <li>Move the threshold. Temperature scaling on the labels cuts calibration error from 0.062 to 0.009 and
        suggests a scaled threshold of 0.711 for 95% precision. Not applied yet, and it needs a second labeller
        and more companies before I trust it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
